for home carer rachel her car is  literally a lifeline her clients depend  on it but keeping on the road is getting  more and more difficult  it's definitely something i've noticed i  filled up only yesterday and it was 77  pound for a whole tank of fuel i can  remember a couple of years back where it  was just 35 pounds for a tank of fuel so  obviously when you're driving your car  you know 300 miles a week and to visit  clients and go and support them in their  own homes you know that all really adds  up and it's quite a lot of miles  rachel's company has had to increase  mileage rates it pays  it's just one sector feeling the strain  as the cost of filling an average family  car rose to a hundred pounds this week  is it getting ridiculous yes it is  getting ridiculous you know with  everybody you know for everybody what  with the rising cost of everything else  as well  it's just a nightmare now you know a lot  of the people i've heard stories from  have been paying to go to work  it used to cost me around 50 60 pounds  for this car and i was about 90 95  pounds four by riding my bike and  everything to work everything which is  an option  new data reveals one in 20 workers say  they wouldn't be able to cover their  living costs for even one week if they  lost their main source of income leading  to many changing how they're getting  around  the number of people taking the bus at  least once a week rose from 12 to the  start of march to 15  by the end of may an extra 1 million  passengers and in the last three months  the number of people cycling has climbed  steeply up 200 percent on certain days  compared to march 2020  in the centre of town the  gloucestershire bike project says it's  seeing more and more people switching  from four wheels to two  it runs projects to donate bikes to  those on the lowest incomes what we have  seen this year and particularly in the  last three months we're getting probably  four or five times the number of  requests that we used to get and we get  a lot of requests anyway you know we  donate hundreds of bikes every year  but the people we're getting them from  we're seeing more people that are in  work on low incomes than we are people  that are out of work on very low incomes  whether it's bikes or buses record  prices at the pumps are having an impact  on how we all get around affordably dan  whitehead sky news in gloucester
 

